overview: do not re-issue set_layer() on re-show — it crashes the shell

Opening the launcher, closing it, and opening it again kills the whole shell.
The compositor answers with

    Gdk-Message: Error 71 (Protocol error) dispatching to Wayland display.

and singularity-desktop exits 1. The session script respawns it, so the symptom
users report is "the desktop resets" rather than a crash. It is fully
deterministic: the SECOND open does it, at any speed. It is not a race, and
adding delay between toggles does not avoid it.

Cause: the overview sets its layer twice.

  overview.vala:59   set_layer(this, GtkLayerShell.Layer.TOP);   // construction
  overview.vala:404  set_layer(this, GtkLayerShell.Layer.TOP);   // every re-show

The layer is only ever TOP -- those are the only two set_layer() calls in the
file -- so the second one changes nothing. But it runs in the show path, which
is reached after hide() has already unmapped the window. gtk4-layer-shell then
issues zwlr_layer_surface_v1.set_layer against the layer surface that was
destroyed at unmap, and the compositor terminates the client for a protocol
violation.

First open works because the window has never been mapped, so the layer surface
from construction is still live. Close unmaps and destroys it. Second open hits
the dead object.

Fix: delete the redundant call. The layer is established once at construction
and never changes, so nothing needs to re-assert it.

Measured on a Radxa Orion O6 (CIX Sky1, kernel 7.2.0-rc7-sky1-ncz), reproducer =
12 toggle_launcher calls over D-Bus at 0.4s intervals: 2 shell restarts before,
and the fix must hold that at 0.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/components/overview/overview.vala
+++ b/src/components/overview/overview.vala
@@ -401,7 +401,12 @@
 
                 // Start invisible, present (map surface), then animate in.
                 opacity = 0;
-                set_layer(this, GtkLayerShell.Layer.TOP);
+                // NOTE: do NOT call set_layer() here. The layer is set once at
+                // construction and never changes. Re-issuing it on a window that
+                // hide() has already unmapped sends zwlr_layer_surface_v1.set_layer
+                // to a destroyed surface, which the compositor answers with a
+                // protocol error and kills the shell -- reproducible as: open the
+                // launcher, close it, open it again.
                 present();
                 // Populate AFTER present so the empty overview (background +
                 // search bar) paints immediately; the grid then streams in
